.TH CCOL_CHANNEL_DESTROY 3 "2026-09-26" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_channel_destroy \- destroy a ccol_channel
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_channel_destroy(ch);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro frees all the resources of the ccol_channel, including both
of the queues inside it, and then sets the ccol_channel pointer to
NULL.

ccol_event_loop_remove() does not wait for a callback that it already
gave to a reactor thread, so such a callback can be inside one of the
queues of the channel when the removal returns. This call blocks until
every one of those callbacks finishes with that queue, and only then
frees the queue. This is what makes the documented teardown sequence
(drain, remove, destroy) safe as written.

A callback that the loop runs for a different registration can run this
whole sequence for this channel, with any num_reactor_threads. A
dispatch that the loop collected for it but did not start yet does not
hold the destroy up, and it never runs its callback after the removal.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
ch   Channel to destroy. The macro sets it to NULL.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
You can call this with a NULL pointer.

The macro evaluates ch exactly once. It must be a modifiable lvalue,
such as a variable or an element of an array, so an expression with a
side effect, such as handles[i++], destroys and clears one element.
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
Messages that stay in one of the directions cause an assertion failure,
so drain both queues before you destroy the channel.
A watcher on one of the directions also causes an assertion failure. A
watcher is a ccol_select() call, a ccol_select_timed() call, or an
ccol_event_loop registration (ccol_selectable_from_chan). Without that
assertion, the waiter would stay linked and point to the mutex of the
queue below the channel, which the destroy is about to free.
Call ccol_event_loop_remove() before you destroy the channel, or let
every ccol_select() call and every ccol_select_timed() call that
watches the channel return first.
Do not call this from inside an ccol_event_loop callback that dispatches
one of the directions of this ccol_channel: the destroy would wait for
that same callback. This is a caller error, and the program stops
through ccol_fatal_err().
Do not hold an application lock across this call if one of these
callbacks takes that lock, because the destroy waits for the callback
while the callback waits for the lock.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_channel_create (3)
